C IRIW+scfences
"Direction 1 (C++20 forbids, C11 allows). Independent-reads-of-independent-writes
 with an SC fence between each reader's two relaxed loads. C++11/14/17 seq_cst
 FENCES are too weak to forbid this (a known defect); C++20 adopted the repaired
 seq_cst of Lahav et al. (P0668), which forbids it. The C++20 side is modelled with
 herd7's RC11 model (rc11.cat), whose repaired-SC fragment is exactly the P0668
 semantics C++20 adopted; the C11 side is herd7's original C11 model.
   herd7 -model rc11.cat     => Never      (C++20's repaired SC forbids)
   herd7 -model c11_orig.cat => Sometimes  (C11/C++17 allow)"
{ int x=0; int y=0; }
P0 (atomic_int* x) { atomic_store_explicit(x,1,memory_order_relaxed); }
P1 (atomic_int* y) { atomic_store_explicit(y,1,memory_order_relaxed); }
P2 (atomic_int* x, atomic_int* y) {
  int r1 = atomic_load_explicit(x,memory_order_relaxed);
  atomic_thread_fence(memory_order_seq_cst);
  int r2 = atomic_load_explicit(y,memory_order_relaxed);
}
P3 (atomic_int* x, atomic_int* y) {
  int r3 = atomic_load_explicit(y,memory_order_relaxed);
  atomic_thread_fence(memory_order_seq_cst);
  int r4 = atomic_load_explicit(x,memory_order_relaxed);
}
exists (2:r1=1 /\ 2:r2=0 /\ 3:r3=1 /\ 3:r4=0)
